===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|Canada|US|also|figuratively}} A [[ceremony]] amongst certain [[indigenous]] [[person|peoples]] of the [[Pacific Northwest]] in which [[gift#Noun|gifts]] are [[bestow]]ed upon [[guest]]s and [[personal property]] is [[destroy]]ed in a [[show#Noun|show]] of [[generosity]] and [[wealth]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles Wilkes|authorlink=Charles Wilkes|chapter=Puget Sound and Okonagan|title=Narrative of the United States Exploring Expedition during the Years 1838, 1839, 1840, 1841, 1842.{{nb...|[...] In Five Volumes, and an Atlas.}}|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{...|Printed by}} C. Sherman|year=1844|volume=IV|page=445|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_8KwbCRdNa3wC/page/n490/mode/1up|oclc=1050156225|passage=First, the Indian himself is to be sought out; then the horse is to be tried; next the price is to be discussed, then the mode of payment, and finally the '''potlatch''': each and all are matters of grave consideration and delay, during which the Indians make a business of watching every circumstance of which they can take advantage. No one can be sure of closing his bargain, until the terms are duly arranged, the '''potlatch''' given, and the horse delivered.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert Brown|chapter=The North-western American Indians|title=The Races of Mankind:{{nb...|Being a Popular Description of the Characteristics, Manners and Customs of the Principal Varieties of the Human Family.}}|location=London; Paris|publisher=[[w:Cassell (publisher)|Cassell, Petter, & Galpin]]|year=1873|volume=I|pages=75–76|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=B1ABAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA75|oclc=913570560|passage=The end of all this scraping and hoarding is to give away the property again at some '''''potlatch''''', at which in a few hours the labour of years will be dissipated. The feasts are often given by the chief men of small tribes as a sort of peace-offering to more powerful ones; but most frequently they are looked upon in the light of gratifying the vanity of the giver and of adding to his personal consequence. [...] The chiefs are under the necessity of frequently giving these '''''potlatches''''' in order to preserve their popularity, just as the old knights used to scatter largess to their followers; [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Franz Boas|authorlink=Franz Boas|title=The Social Organization and the Secret Societies of the Kwakiutl Indians,{{nb...|by Franz Boas. Based on Personal Observations and on Notes Made by Mr. George Hunt.}}|journal=Annual Report of the Board of Regents of the {{w|Smithsonian Institution}}, {{...|Showing the Operations, Expenditures, and Condition of the Institution for the Year Ending June 30, 1895.}} Report of the U.S. National Museum|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|Government Printing Office]]|year=1897|section=chapter XIII (The Religious Ceremonials of Other Tribes of the North Pacific Coast)|pages=647–648|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-A4jAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA3-PA648|issn=0097-644X|oclc=183317757|passage=Nusk’Elu′sta, the Indian, to whom I owe my information regarding the clans, and who is a member of the gens Ialô′stimōt of the Taliô′mx·, stated that he had received the raven [carving] when he gave his first '''potlatch'''. At his second '''potlatch''' he received the eagle. He hoped that his mother would give him the whale at his next '''potlatch''', and would at the same time divulge to him the secrets connected with it. [...] Property is also destroyed at '''potlatches'''. This is not returned, and serves only to enhance the social position of the individual who performed this act.}}
#* {{RQ:London Son of the Wolf|page=162|passage=That night there was a grand wedding and a '''''potlach'''''; so that for two days to follow there was no fishing done by the village.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Viola E[dmundson] Garfield|authorlink=Viola Garfield|chapter=Part 1: The Tsimshian and Their Neighbors|mainauthor=Viola E. Garfield; Paul S. Wingert; {{w|Marius Barbeau}}|editor=Marian W. Smith|title=The Tsimshian: Their Arts and Music|series=Publications of the {{w|American Ethnological Society}}|seriesvolume=XVIII|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=J. J. Augustin|year=[1951]|section=chapter III (Political Organization)|page=37|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4oRsG693SYkC&pg=PA37|oclc=1154506152|passage=No '''potlatch''' or secret society initiation could be given by a tribal member until the chief had officially opened the winter ceremonial season. His affairs took priority over those sponsored by lineage heads, who must provide him with wealth befor they were able to accumulate for their own '''potlatches'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David Guterson|authorlink=David Guterson|chapter=7|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fUcYLRN8smcC&pg=PT74|title={{w|Snow Falling on Cedars}}|location=[Boston, Mass.]|publisher={{w|Houghton Mifflin Harcourt}}|date=12 September 1994|year_published=2012|isbn=978-0-547-54508-0|passage=One part bacchanal, one part tribal '''potlatch''', one part vestigial New England supper, the entire affair hinged on the coronation of the Strawberry Princess—always a virginal Japanese maiden dressed in satin and dusted carefully across the face with rice powder—in an oddly solemn ceremony before the Island County Courthouse at sundown of the inaugural evening.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael Sorkin|authorlink=Michael Sorkin|chapter=War is Swell|editor=Donald Albrecht|title=World War II and the American Dream: How Wartime Building Changed a Nation|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|National Building Museum}}|year=1995|page=251|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TrBKFjC5GkcC&pg=PA1940|isbn=978-0-262-01145-7|passage=This was the delusion of Reaganomics, the supply-siders' inability to distinguish between spending and prosperity with the result that we simply waged economic warfare on ourselves in a '''potlatch''' of mutually assured bankruptcy.}}
# {{lb|en|US|chiefly|Alaska}} A [[communal]] [[meal]] to which guests bring [[dish#Noun|dishes]] to [[share#Verb|share]]; a [[potluck]].
#: {{synonyms|en|fuddle|q1=Britain, dialectal}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Yuval Noah Harari|authorlink=Yuval Noah Harari|title={{w|Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Vintage Books}}|year=2014|isbn=978-0-09-959008-8|passage=If all went according to plan, the bands could harvest tons of meat, fat and animal skins in a single afternoon of collective effort, and either consume these riches in a giant '''potlatch''', or dry, smoke or (in Arctic areas) freeze them for later usage.}}

